John H. wrote:
> Fantasy Nethack Variant

I'm mostly playing slash'em but also intend to write port of some of 
theses patches...

> My own fantasy version of Nethack, composed entirely out of
> currently-existing patches, would contain this:
> 
> Moving Clouds (makes the clouds on the Plane of Air move like the water
> bubbles do on the Plane of Water)
> Nymphs in Trees (for more variety in tree kick results, and is cool)
> Cancelling Traps (Cancellation charges are valuable, in a way that
> exceeds the benefit from destroying the trap, so I say it's balanced.
>  I would remove the ability to destroy magic portals, however.)

Yep, these 3 patches seems nice.

> Biodiversity (many cool monsters and you can never have enough of
> those; however, I'd remove the special dragon names as unnecessarily
> confusing, and maybe some other aspects as well)

I'm not quite sure about this one. I'm afraid the description (on the 
database) is a bit too short for me to have a good idea oin what it does 
and whether I'll like it...

> AoY Container (makes identification possibly useful even if all major
> item classes are ID'd, by removing the major ways of differentiating
> the Amulet from an imitation without it)

Agree.

> Assorted YAFM's (Specifically, for the message if you win while
> hallucinating.)

Mostly cosmetic. But might be funny anyway :-)

> Bag of Tricks Enhancement (turns an item without much to recommend it
> beyond an alias for a Wand of Create Monster and confusion with bags
> into something a bit more interesting, without making it too useful)

Not on my top-priority list of patch to have. May be funny.

> Color Alchemy (very cool idea)

Agree. Really cool idea. Might twist game balance because of allowing to 
alchemise far much potion types. But the most interesting one are 
already alchemisable, so...

> DSM Cancellation (logical)

Yep.

> Dungeon Growths (lets herb foodtypes and trees grow and spread as time
> passes; they even grow while you're not on a level!  I don't think
> it checks trees to make sure the level doesn't become impassible,
> though.)

Again, I'll need a bit more information that what's on the patch 
database on this one.

> Invisible Objects (cool, logical)

Already done in [s].

> Free Fortune Cookie (funny)

Yep, funny.

> Garden (new room types are cool)

Yes.

> Grudge (very cool)

Seems really nice but also a bit restricted (few monsters types 
attacking each other). Some could be added, there should also be 
something for monsters of a race hating the player race.

> Heck 1d Heck2 or Lethe (Heck1 for random mazes with special sections,
> Heck2 for hand-drawn mazes, and Lethe adds special levels, amnesia
> water and some new monsters... I'd probably tone down pet rebellion a
> bit, though)

I'm playing with the slash'em Lethe patch (googlegroups slashem lethe to 
find it again). I've just reached the castle for the first Lethe-time... 
Changes thingsd a lot. Really nice.

> Magical Lenses (cool, extends an underused object category)

Agree.

> Lever (only if special levels are made using it, though)

Yes. Moreover, I'm persuaded that we could use levers to build Soko-like 
puzzles.

> Improved Beartraps (logical, also makes Wands of Opening more useful
> without them being *too* useful)

Right.

> New Bigrooms, New Castle Levels, New Medusa Levels, New Sokoban Levels
> (for variety is the spice of life)

Already some of them in [s].

> Pet Ranged Attack (a long time in coming, and helps removes part of the
> sense with many monsters that, once you've tamed them, they become
> synonyms for each other with different numbers of hitdice)

Agree. That's the first patch I've adapted for [s]. Seing your black 
dragon, at least, breathing disintegration, or your elf not dropping its 
bow is a rather nice feeling.


I would also add the almost vital sortloot patch (actually the patch 
that made me compile my own version because I needed it so much...) and 
the darkroom patch (so usefull in the mines...)


I'm also playing with my "reduce spell hunger" patch to reduce spell 
hunger of heavy spellcaster (a bit wizard-like), especially done for 
mages. But I'm not too happy with it right now and I'll rewrite it as 
soon as my current game is over in order to take into account some kind 
of familiarity with the spell (that is flame mages would have reduce 
spell hunger for fire-based spells but increase for water-based).

And I've also a bunch of (more or less) personnal YANI to implement 
